    script: "Host: Today's topic is whether gig platform workers, like rideshare and delivery drivers, should be reclassified as employees rather than independent contractors. I'm joined by labour advocate Victor Alameda and platform economist Simone Bissett. Victor, make your case. Victor: Contractor status leaves drivers without minimum wage guarantees, paid sick leave, or employer contributions to benefits, even though platforms control pricing, routes, and can deactivate accounts unilaterally. That level of control looks a lot like employment, and workers should get corresponding protections. Host: Simone, you see risks in reclassification. Simone: Reclassifying every gig worker as an employee would likely force platforms to cut the number of active drivers, since scheduling shifts costs more than an on-demand pool. Surveys show a large share of drivers actually value the flexibility to log on and off freely, and that flexibility is harder to preserve under standard employment rules. Victor: Some hybrid models exist, though — a few jurisdictions guarantee minimum earnings and benefits contributions while still allowing flexible scheduling. That shows the trade-off isn't as rigid as it first appears. Simone: True, those hybrid models are promising, but they've mostly been tested at small scale, and it's unclear whether they hold up once applied broadly across an entire industry. Host: Where might this land? Victor: I'd support piloting the hybrid model city by city, with real earnings and benefits floors, before mandating full employee status everywhere. Simone: I could accept that too, as long as the pilots are evaluated on both worker income and whether enough driving capacity remains for riders. If flexibility disappears entirely, everyone loses.",
