    script: "Host: The city council is considering a congestion charge for vehicles entering downtown during peak hours. With me are policy analyst Aisha Rahman and commuter advocate Ray Delgado. Aisha, why support this? Aisha: Cities that introduced congestion pricing saw meaningful drops in downtown traffic and faster bus travel times almost immediately, because a portion of drivers shift to transit, cycling, or off-peak travel once there's a cost attached to peak-hour driving. The revenue raised typically funds transit improvements too. Host: Ray, you represent commuters who'd be affected. Ray: My worry is fairness. Many suburban commuters don't have a realistic transit option for their route, so this charge would simply become another cost squeezed out of people who have no alternative, while downtown residents who can walk or cycle pay nothing. Aisha: That's a legitimate gap, and cities that got this right paired the charge with expanded park-and-ride service and discounted transit passes for commuters from underserved suburbs, phased in before the charge started. Ray: That would help, but timing matters — if the charge arrives before decent transit options exist, commuters just absorb the cost with no way to change their behaviour. Aisha: Agreed, sequencing is critical. I'd support delaying the charge until at least basic park-and-ride infrastructure is running, rather than launching both simultaneously. Ray: I could support that. My other request would be a lower rate, or an exemption, for commuters earning below a certain income threshold, since a flat charge affects people very differently depending on income. Aisha: That's worth studying — income-based discounts have worked in a few other cities without undermining the traffic reduction goal.",
